course: dictionary
lesson: 14728
title: Big, plentiful words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Profuse» means coming or given in very large amounts, or growing in great numbers. You can use it when there is far more of something than you expected. Look for clues in the words around it.
explain: «The cut caused profuse bleeding, so the nurse pressed a clean cloth on it.» Bleeding means blood coming out, and profuse tells you there was a lot of it. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Try a big amount, like lots of, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «She gave profuse thanks to the lady who found her dog.» Here profuse means thanks given again and again, very freely. The clue is the thanks: someone very grateful may say thank you many times. The same word can mean different things, so always check the context.
fact: Profuse means coming or given in very large amounts.
fact: «Profuse» can mean given many times, as in «profuse apologies» or «profuse thanks».
fact: «Profuse» can mean growing thickly and in great numbers, as in «profuse flowers».
fact: «Profuse bleeding» means a lot of blood is coming out, so it is a warning sign in first aid.
fact: Profuse is an adjective: it describes a noun, such as bleeding, thanks or flowers.
exercise: «The cut caused profuse bleeding.» What does profuse mean here? | Dry and clean | Painless | Very little | *A large amount, pouring out
exercise: «He gave profuse apologies after he spilt the juice.» What does profuse mean? | Written in pencil | *Given very many times and freely | Given once and quickly | Not said at all
exercise: «The garden had profuse roses along every wall.» Which word means growing in great numbers? | roses | garden | wall | *profuse
test: «The nurse saw profuse bleeding and called for help.» What does profuse show? | *A lot of blood coming out | A small scratch | A broken bone | Blood that had dried up
test: Mia says profuse means tiny. What does it really mean? | Tiny and hard to see | Very slow and quiet | *Coming or given in very large amounts | Sad and tired
exam: «The shop owner gave profuse thanks, and the children felt proud.» What does profuse mean in «profuse thanks»? | *Given very many times and freely | Written down in a book | Given once, and quietly | Lost and found again
